< {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"item/completed","params":{"sessionId":"01a1c720-6e9e-7431-8326-418afccd04df","viewCursor":"v9:01a1c720-6e9e-7431-8326-418afccd04df:11","sourceRange":{"stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1c720-6e9e-7431-8326-418afccd04df"},"first":{"id":"01a1c720-8c15-7671-9bb4-dd50e90aa7d3","sequence":67},"last":{"id":"01a1c720-8c15-7671-9bb4-dd50e90aa7d3","sequence":67}},"item":{"itemId":"d3c0efa1-28ae-4874-8a10-b12fb226699a","kind":"agentMessage","turnId":"01a1c720-7065-771e-9acf-d8472c711f3b","revision":2,"status":"completed","recordedAt":"2026-11-10T20:22:41.429455Z","text":"Rain forms when moisture in the air gathers, cools, and falls back to Earth as liquid water. It begins with evaporation, as heat from the sun turns water from oceans, lakes, and soil into invisible vapor that rises. As the vapor climbs, air pressure and temperature drop, causing condensation: the vapor clings to tiny dust, salt, or smoke particles and becomes microscopic droplets that form clouds. Inside clouds, droplets collide and merge, growing heavier until air currents can no longer support them. When they become large enough, gravity pulls them downward as rain. Cooler temperatures aloft may first create ice crystals that melt into raindrops while falling. This continuous movement, called the water cycle, redistributes fresh water across the planet.","messagePhase":"finalAnswer"}},"emittedAtMs":1794342161434}
